version 0.9.1
schematic power-domains

# Case 3 of the ten (AGENTS.md §10, docs/backlog.md F12): twenty power domains, one table.
#
# The shape is the point. Twenty regulated rails, five devices each, one hundred instances, and
# two nets that carry the whole board: `VDD_IN` with sixty members and `GND` with eighty. A power
# tree is the case that asks whether the table form survives repetition, and it does -- every row
# is one fact, the two wide nets wrap into continuation lines under the 80-column rule, and the
# board reads as a hundred short rows a reviewer can scan. No expression is needed to say any of
# it, so ADR-0001 D6's trigger is not met by this case.
#
# This is not a claim that hand-writing this file is the way to build a power tree. The repetition
# is what generators are for (ADR-0001 D3: generators live outside the language) and what modules
# are for (ADR-0013). What the case pins is the *form* at this scale, so that the answer to
# `AGENTS.md` §11 Q2 rests on a case instead of on an opinion.
